A||||8||Rick 88||Dropped Front Lower Control Arms???????||||5||
Z||000000||Rick 88||10-03-2001||10:57 PM||||There is somebody out there in Fieroland who is making dropped lower control arms for our cars. I found the site once but I didn't bookmark it. I have seen this used on Sport Trucks. Anyone have these, or know where the site is? How about opinions? You retain the stock spring and spindle. The spring perch in the lower control arm is redone to allow the spring to sit lower in the arm itself.<BR>Hmmmmmmm  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_brow.gif"><P>Rick 88||||reg||5
Z||000001||FieroHarry||10-03-2001||11:18 PM||||Rick maybe im wrong but that wouldnt work.<BR>The spindle height gets changed to lower the car.<BR>If you changed the position of the spring in just the lower control arm then it would just wobble around up top....||||reg||1
Z||000002||Tigger||10-03-2001||11:38 PM||||That apprach would require an entire new LCA, probably tubular with the lower spring seat welded in lower in the arm.  My guess is that new LCA's with bushings and ball joints would probably cost just as much as dropped spindles.<P>It would work, but IMO it's probably not the best approach for the Fiero.<p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 10-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Rick 88||10-04-2001||02:59 PM||||I'm not sure how they work but I have seen them on full size trucks. If I can find the guy that makes them for Fieros I'll post the site. Somebody else has had to have see it.<P>Rick 88||||reg||1
Z||000004||Jimmy||10-04-2001||03:52 PM||||I remember that they used caddy ball joints on the conversion also.  I can't seem to remember who did the conversion either.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Jim<BR>87 GT T-TOP V8<BR>87 GT||||reg||1
Z||000005||dman||10-04-2001||04:06 PM||||Hello everyone.<P>The web site you mention is probably this one:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.shelbycustom.com/addons/addons.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.shelbycustom.com/addons/addons.html</A> <P>The usage of caddilac ball joints as a means of lowering was mentioned on this web site. If perform a search (you may need to hit the tech archives) I believe you'll find several posts. I believe it was lowCG who brought this idea to the forums attention. I believe Spyder also ended up using this technique for his 355 replica.<P>Cheers, dman.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Raydar||10-04-2001||06:26 PM||||I used to have "drop" lower control arms on my Sonoma. Worked okay, except that they were manufactured poorly (Chisholm suspension, BTW). Weren't symmetrical. Once they were installed, the left side alignment only required minor tweeking. The right side required a huge amount of adjustment. Truck was really squirrely going over bumps.<BR>I ended up having them removed and replaced with drop spindles.<BR>I suspect that if they had been manufactured correctly, they would have been fine.<P><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Rick 88||10-04-2001||09:55 PM||||dman,<P>Thanks for the site location. What would the difference be between using drop control arms or drop spindles. The spindles for 88's from Held widen the front track. This is not something I find appealing given the 88's already wider front track. Are there any other drop spindle producers for 88's?<P>Rick 88||||reg||1
